COMMIT 7dc05550240b28586de31b6b58eb4ba9750faaa5
ci(ledger): guard the ledger tables on cell shape, never on pipe count

FIX-132 fixed eleven malformed rows, ten of which were losing text silently on
GitHub, and then the defect came back twice in one day: a closing pipe dropped
while editing FIX-71's row, and two raw pipes written into FIX-132's own
evidence cell while describing how to escape them. This is the guard, and the
distinction in the subject line is the whole of it. FIX-54, FIX-56 and FIX-57
each carried the header's pipe count and seven cells, because a stray separator
was paid for by a missing closing pipe. The pipe count I had offered as proof
called all three clean while GitHub ate their evidence cells. Counting pipes is
not counting cells, so the check splits on unescaped pipes and compares cell
shape: BACKLOG exactly the header's count, DECISIONS at most, because its 32
legacy Reason-less rows render as an empty cell and lose nothing while an
over-count loses text. Both must close. A missing ledger file is a failure, not
a pass -- a guard that exits 0 when its subject has been renamed asserts
something false, which is worse than no guard.

The red was made to mean something before the suite was written. A Python suite
against a module that does not exist reds once, with ModuleNotFoundError, and
that says nothing about whether any case can fail for its own reason. So the
test-author wrote the implementation first as a deliberately wrong stub that
exits 0 for every input, and each of the 19 cases then failed on its own
assertion with its fixture named.

Review r1 FAILed it, correctly, on a case that could not fail under any
mutation: aligned separators above an all-good fixture exits 0 whether the
separator is recognised or not. The test killed nothing, and what it hid was
worse than its own emptiness -- a ledger using aligned separators would have
been checked not at all, silently, with a green tick. It now sits above a bad
row, so it proves the table opened. Two further gaps the reviewer named as
surviving mutants are closed (a well-formed row with trailing and leading
whitespace; a header row without its closing pipe), and one dead clause is
deleted rather than left standing in for documentation.

All seven of the reviewer's mutants were then run by the verifier and killed,
each by the case the reviewer named. One of those results was false, and the
cause is now a rule in NOTES rather than a story: two Python mutants of the
same byte length, written within the same second, reuse the first one's cached
bytecode, so the suite literally executes the other mutant's code. It was
caught only because the reported failing case belonged to the other mutant.
Clear __pycache__ and set PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, or a Python mutant result
is not evidence. Java is unaffected, since Maven recompiles.

The guard earned its row inside the hour. Writing FIX-143 I put a raw pipe into
a cell and it answered "agent/BACKLOG.md:228: expected 6 cells, found 7" --
the FIX-132 defect, mine, caught by the check built for it instead of by a
reader noticing words had gone missing.

Evidence: 19/19 OK, exit 0 on the real ledgers, and the integrated gate run
over this tree and FIX-140's together -- clean verify -Pit exit 0, BUILD
SUCCESS, 12:00, 17 modules SUCCESS, every Tests run line at zero failures,
errors and skips, no blocked platform thread, coverage "no decrease".

